## Introduction
Along with the fast depletion of fossil fuel, the urgent demand for sustainable energy and carbon neutrality has been witnessed over the last decades [l-4]. Aiming at these tasks, various energy storage and conversion devices have emerged. Recently, electrochemical energy storage, as one of the essential parts of clean energy, has derived many promising devices such as lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) and super capacitors (SCs), which play vital roles in portable devices and vehicles [5-7]. Especially, owing to the high power density, fast charge/discharge rate, and long cycle life, SCs are considered as promising energy storage devices for the next-generation electronics [5,8,9]. Typically, the electrochemical double layer (EDL） and the redox reactions (called pseudocapacitance) are the most common mechanism for SCs to store electrical energy. The EDL is formed by the electrolyte ions on the surface of the electrode, while the pseudocapacitance involves the electron transfer between the electrolyte and the surface regions of electrode materials [9-1l]. It could be noted that for either charge-storing mechanism, the electrode materials are the key factors determining the energy storage performance [6,9,12]. Recently, various carbon-based materials, including activated carbon [l3], carbide-derived carbons [14]， carbon nanotubes [15, 16],and graphene [17], have been utilized for SCs applications given to their high electrical conductivity and high surface area [l8,19]. Nevertheless, to widen the transformative advances of SCs, not only developing processing or synthetic techniques for high-performance materials, but also revealing the charge/discharge behavior and guiding the design of functional electrodes should be expected. Hence, except for the carbon materials, entirely new classes of active electrode materials especially with well-defined structures have been more and more developed and studied.
Mostly, the superior power density of SCs is drawn from the interactions between the electrolytes and the electrodes [20]. Materials that combine proper functional groups and structures are required for the robust performance,which determines the electrical conductivity and surface area, respectively [21, 22]. Metalorganic frameworks (MOFs), as the porous crystalline materials constructed from organic linkers and metal knots, contain surpassing characteristics such as high porosity and tunable compositions [23]. MOFs have been widely used for various applications including water absorption, gas separation and storage, catalytic applications, etc. Yet, due to the electrically insulating nature of most conventional MOFs, their use in SCs has been limited as precursors for carbon templates or for hybrid composites by mixing with conductive additives [5]. Very recently, electricallyconductiveMOFsand pristine MOF-based electrodes have emerged and explored in electrochemical applications [ll, 24]. By combining potential pseudocapacitive sites and large specific areas in their structures, MOFs hold the capability to be used as a promising option for electrode materials [25]. Especially, electrolyte ions can be adsorbed at the interface between MOFs-electrolyte through diffusion, forming efficient EDL for the SCs. For example, Zheng and co-workers have reported a nanoscale MOF with uncommon semiconductor behavior $(\sigma=4.2\times10^{-3}~\mathrm{{S}{\cdot}\mathrm{{m}^{-1})}}$ and high specific capacitance of $1{,}927{.}14\ \mathrm{F{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ [26]. The periodic functionality of MOFs allows the installation of accessible functional sites along with the entire framework, promoting the charging and discharging performance of MOFs-based electrodes. Thus, it is very necessary and instructive to construct the relationships between the structural features and performance by rationally designing the functional groups that interact with the electrolyte ions.
In this work, aiming at revealing the relationships between functional groups of MOFs and the performance of the EDL drove SCs,we designed and synthesized three Eu-MOFs for the fabrication of SCs electrodes, which were named as $\mathrm{Eu}_{2}$ (5-methylm-phthalic acid) $\mid_{3}$ $\left(\mathrm{Eu-CH}_{3}\right)$ ， $\mathrm{Eu}_{2}$ (5-hydroxymethylisophthalate) $_3(\mathrm{Eu-CH_{2}O H})$ ,and $\mathrm{Eu}_{2}(1,3\cdot$ -benzenedicarboxylic acid 5-aminomethyl-hydrochloride)( $\mathrm{Eu-CH_{2}N H_{3}C l})$ ，respectively. Notably, all of the Eu-MOFs contained the same crystalline phase, indicating the same pore and surface structures. Meanwhile, benefiting from the tunable features, each Eu-MOFs installed different functional groups that generated the corresponding affinity with electrolyte. Once used as the active electrodes, the asfabricated EDL SCs with different MOFs demonstrated distinguished performance. Combining with the theoretical calculation, we successfuly clarified the general charge storage mechanism related to the material structure and the influence of molecular dynamics on capacitance properties. Besides, our work explored the potentials of well-defined MOFs as the model materials for the instructional construction of electrode materials.
