## 3.1 Characterization of the MOF-based electrodes
The multifunctional groups were also clearly verified by the Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectroscopy (Fig.l(c)). For the $\mathrm{Eu-CH}_{3},$ the C-H stretching vibration peak of $\mathrm{-CH}_{3}$ could be found near 2,948 and $2,865~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ 。 while the related bending vibration peak was observed in the range of $1,465{-}1,440\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ .For the infrared bands of Eu- ${\mathrm{CH}}_{2}{\mathrm{OH}}.$ the stretching vibration peaks of C-O and $_\mathrm{O-H}$ clearly found at 1,032 and $3{,}440\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ were the stretching vibration peaks of $\mathrm{C-O}$ and $_\mathrm{O-H}$ ，respectively. The stretching vibration peaks of C-N and N-H at 1,ll0 and 3,418 $c\mathbf{m}^{-1}$ verified the functional groups of ] $\begin{array}{r}{\mathtt{E u-C H_{2}N H_{3}C l.}}\end{array}$ Besides, the SEM-associated elemental mapping by energy-dispersive $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray spectroscopy (EDS) (Figs. S3-S5 in the ESM) demonstrated the homogeneous distribution of $\mathrm{C},$ O, and Eu along each MOFs. Moreover, the l $\mathrm{Eu-CH_{2}N H_{3}C l}$ also contained homogeneously distributed N and Cl elements. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra confirmed the composition of Eu-MOFs (Figs. S6-S8 in the ESM). For each MOFs, the Eu element displayed two peaks corresponding to Eu $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ and Eu $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ [28,29]. The C 1s spectrum was deconvoluted into four peaks assigned to different carbon bonding states, including C-C $(\mathsf{s p}^{2})$ (from the benzene ring),C-C $(\mathsf{s p}^{3})$ (from the methyl), and $scriptstyle\mathrm{O=C-O}$ (from the carboxylate functionalities） [30]. The O ls region of the XPS spectra suggested two different bound states for oxygen, O-C and $\mathrm{C=O}_{\mathrm{i}}$ 、representing the carboxylates in the structure. Specially, for $\mathrm{Eu-CH}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{Cl},$ the N ls peak could be attributed to $\mathrm{C-NH}_{2}$ and the Cl 2p spectra displayed two peaks corresponding to Cl $2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}$ and Cl $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ (Fig. S8 in the ESM) [31].
Thus, benefiting from the tunable nature, these as-designed EuMOFs realized the installation of multifunctional groups on the same framework. The different functional groups not only could modulate the approachable areas of the electrodes during the charge/discharge process. Theoretically, affected by the structural organization, the specific surface areas of $\operatorname{Eu-CH}_{3},$ Eu- $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH},$ and $\mathrm{Eu-CH}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{Cl}$ were calculated as 564.835,446.495,and $199.51\mathrm{m^{2}{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ 、respectively (the calculated method is detailed in the ESM). Furthermore, the functional groups with different polarity would lead to distinguished affinity to the electrolyte and the structure of MOFs would lead to considerable stability (Fig.S9 in the ESM). Herein, we prepared two electrolytes, tetraethylammonium tetrafluoroborate $(\mathrm{TEABF_{4}})$ and ammonium tetraethyl chloride (TEACl), which were quite different in structural size and electronegativity. Notably, the $\mathrm{TEABF_{4}}$ was larger even than the pore size,while TEACl possessed smaller sizes and the same composition as the $\mathrm{Eu-CH_{2}N H_{3}C l}.$ 、During the charge/discharge process, the diverse interactions between the function groups $\left(\mathrm{-CH}_{3},\right.$ $-\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH},$ and $\mathrm{-CH_{2}N H_{3}C l)}$ and the electrolyte affected the formation of EDL, resulting into distinguished performance (vide infra).
## 3.2 Charging/discharging process
In order to gain accurate comparison, the as-obtained MOFs powders were directly used as the electrodes without conductive additives. During the test, a typical three-electrode cell with carbon cloth as the current collector was used to evaluate the performance. In the 1 M TEABF4/acetonitrile (ACN) electrolyte, the cycling voltammetry (CV) curves at the sweep speed of $50\ \mathrm{\mV{\cdot}s^{-1}}$ (vs. $\mathrm{\Ag/Ag^{+}})$ (Figs. 2(a)-2(c)) and the galvanostatic charge and discharge (GCD) tests (Figs. 2(d)-2(f)） of each EuMOFs based electrodes displayed approximately rectangular and triangular traces, respectively, confirming the EDL capacitive behavior. Meanwhile, the CV and GCD tests determined the voltage window of each electrode, revealing a working potential of $\sim1\mathrm{V}$ The CV curves at different scan rates (Fig.Si0 in the ESM) demonstrate semi-rectangular shapes, further indicating the typical EDL storage mechanism. Accordingly, the current density increased with the increase of the scan rates, suggesting the considerable rate performance. The GCD properties at different current densities also displayed triangular profiles (Fig. S1O in the ESM) due to the electrostatic charge storage mechanism. The specific capacitances of each multifunctional electrode at different current densities were calculated (Fig. Sll in the ESM). At $2\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ 386.53,335.57, and $332.47\mathrm{Fcdotg^{-1}}$ could be achieved for Eu ${\mathrm{.CH}}_{2}{\mathrm{OH}},$ $\operatorname{Eu-CH}_{3},$ and Eu $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{\mathrm{-CH}}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{Cl},}\end{array}$ respectively. Obviously, even sharing similar framework, the capacitance values in the 1 M TEABF $_{\mathrm{~4~}}^{\mathrm{~1~}}$ ACN electrolyte followed the rules that Eu- ${\mathrm{.CH}}_{2}{\mathrm{OH}}>$ Eu$\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{CH}_{3}\approx\mathrm{Eu}{\mathrm{-CH}_{2}}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{Cl}.}\end{array}$ It should be noted that the ionic diameters of TEA+ with $\mathrm{BF_{4}}^{-}$ were larger than the pore sizes of each EuMOFs. Thus, such distinguished phenomenon proved the role of functional groups in the EDL SCs. During the charge/discharge process, since the OH groups contained stronger interaction with the $\mathrm{BF_{4}}^{-}$ in the electrolyte, the Eu $\mathrm{\Omega_{1-CH_{2}O H}}$ electrodes were more conducive to the formation of a double electric layer. On the contrary, the Eu $\mathrm{\Omega}_{1-\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{Cl}}$ contained cation/anion pairs. The coordination competition between $\mathrm{Cl^{-}}$ and the $\mathrm{BF_{4}}^{-}$ prevented the formation of EDL at the interface between the electrode and electrolyte. These results were also accordant with the electrochemical impedance tests (Fig. Sl2 in the ESM). As shown in Figs. S12 and Sl3 in the ESM, both the ohmic resistance and the charge transfer resistance of the $\mathrm{Eu-CH_{2}O H}$ electrode (10.1 and $1.59\Omega$ 、respectively) were significantly lower than those of Eu $\mathrm{\cdotCH}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{Cl}$ (11.6 and $4.0~\Omega.$ 、respectively) and the $\mathrm{Eu-CH}_{3}$ (10.3 and $4.0\Omega,$ respectively).
We further performed the evaluation in the 1 M TEACl/ACN electrolyte to elucidate the regulatory functions of the wel-defined structures, especially the coordination competition during the charging process. Accordingly, under the typical three-electrode tests,the CV curves (Fig.Sl5 in the ESM) also demonstrated approximately rectangular and triangular traces, respectively. Thus, the double-layer electrostatic energy storage mechanism could be ensured. Since chloride ion possessed a much smaller size than $\mathrm{BF_{4}^{-}}$ 。the dynamic effect dominated stronger influence than the electric field effect at low current density, leading to obvious leakage current and incomplete discharge behavior (Fig. Sl6 in the ESM).At $10\ \mathrm{A{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ , the gravimetric capacitances of $\operatorname{Eu-CH}_{3},$ Eu$\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH},$ andEu $\mathrm{\mathrm{-CH}}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{Cl}$ were calculated as 245.3, 198.8l,and $247.9\mathrm{Fcdotg^{-1}}$ respectively. In particular, the capacitance values in the $1\mathrm{M}$ TEACl/ACN electrolyte obeyed the rules that $\mathrm{Eu-CH_{2}N H_{3}C l}$ $>\mathrm{Eu-CH_{3}}>\mathrm{Eu-CH_{2}O H}$ .For $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{Eu-CH}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{Cl},}\end{array}$ with $\mathrm{Cl^{-}}$ instead of $\mathrm{BF_{4}}^{-}$ 。coordination competition between the functional group and electrolyte was eliminated. Moreover, the large amount of $\mathrm{Cl^{-}}$ in TEACl could benefit the formation of EDLs on $\mathrm{Eu-CH_{2}N H_{3}C l},$ endowing the superior capacitance properties. For Eu- $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ andEu$\mathrm{CH_{2}O H}.$ they obeyed the rules that larger surface generated larger capacity. Hence, with well-defined structures and multifunctional groups, these as designed MOFs based electrodes elucidated that the formation of the double layer electric was dependent on the adsorption and dynamic of ions in electrolyte during the charging process.
## 3.3 Theory calculations
The theoretical study based on density functional theory (DFT) was employed to accurately reveal the interaction between electrolyte ions and electrode materials and elucidate the effects on the performance of super capacitors. Given to the large structures of each MOFs, all calculations were carried on CP2K/Quickstep (detailed information for the calculations could be found in the ESM). The adsorption energy of $\mathrm{BF_{4}}^{-}$ on each of the three electrode materials was employed during the calculations (Fig.4 and Fig. S17 in the ESM). Through the comparison of the bond energies of $\mathrm{BF}_{4}{\mathrm{-CH}}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{BF}_{4}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2},$ it could be found that the adsorption energy of $\mathrm{BF_{4}}^{-}$ on Eu- $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}$ was much higher than the others, indicating that $\mathrm{BF_{4}^{\mathrm{~-~}}}$ could dissociate and adsorb stably on $\mathrm{Eu-CH_{2}O H}$ .In addition, Eu- $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}$ demonstrated the highest adsorption energy for $\mathrm{BF_{4}^{-}}$ ，which also indicated that Eu $-\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}$ could accommodate more ions than the other two materials. According to the theoretical analysis, it could be clearly deduced that the electrodes with stronger interaction with the dynamic of ions in electrolyte could form more efficient double layer electricity during the charging process, which was consistent with the experimental phenomenon and confirmed the findings.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, with rationally designed and prepared Eu-MOF electrodes, we revealed the influence of specific surface area of the electrodes and the interactions between electrodes and electrolyte on the capacitance properties of MOF based super capacitors. Specially, it could be deduced that the larger specific surface area and stronger binding energy between the electrodes and the electrolyte ions would lead to the higher specific capacitance of the final assembled super capacitors. Nevertheless, it should also be noted that too strong binding energy may result in retarded desorption during the charge-discharge process and poor rate performance. All of these findings seem to be universal for the traditional super capacitors. However, it is very necessary to verify these traditional acknowledgments on the newly developing and promising materials. Since MOF-derived super capacitors could make full use of the adjustable advantages of MOFs, they have been regarded as the most valuable materials for next generation energy storage materials and hold promising potentials for variable applications. Our work not only extends the family of MOF-based super capacitors. More importantly, our findings are instructive for the design and selection of MOFs for energy storage applications.
